1
00:00:00,847 --> 00:00:08,823
Welcome to the Europe capitals, episode five of six. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:20,787
Eight capitals of the Balkans, from Albania and Kosovo in the south-west to
Serbia, Montenegro, North Macedonia, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Bulgaria and
Romania.

3
00:00:21,444 --> 00:00:23,313
Let's bring Tirana into view.

4
00:00:27,534 --> 00:00:33,583
Tirana stands on the Erzen river in the centre of Albania, in the valley of a
chain of hills.

5
00:00:34,245 --> 00:00:45,669
Where the name comes from is uncertain; it grew into a town after an Ottoman
general, Sulejman Pasha, built a mosque and a bazaar here in 1614.

6
00:00:46,323 --> 00:00:58,177
Remember Tirana as the town that grew around a mosque and a bazaar of 1614,
and whose grey communist blocks were painted in bright colours from 2000.

7
00:00:58,855 --> 00:01:01,537
Let's bring Pristina into view.

8
00:01:04,549 --> 00:01:11,503
Pristina lies in the centre of Kosovo, on the Prishtevka river, in the middle
of the Balkans.

9
00:01:12,162 --> 00:01:18,002
Where the name comes from is disputed; one reading ties it to a Slavic word
for a stream.

10
00:01:18,659 --> 00:01:30,130
Remember Pristina for the NEWBORN monument, repainted every year, and the
National Library of 1982 covered in 99 domes and a metal mesh.

11
00:01:30,819 --> 00:01:34,314
Let's bring Belgrade into view.

12
00:01:36,564 --> 00:01:43,298
Belgrade stands where the Sava meets the Danube in north-central Serbia, at
the meeting of two great rivers.

13
00:01:43,952 --> 00:01:51,499
Beograd, "white city". It was the capital of Yugoslavia for most of the 20th
century.

14
00:01:52,162 --> 00:02:01,671
Remember Belgrade as the white city at the meeting of the Sava and the Danube,
with the Kalemegdan fortress above the confluence.

15
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:04,858
Let's bring Podgorica into view.

16
00:02:08,085 --> 00:02:17,129
Podgorica lies on the Morača river in central Montenegro, on the Zeta plain
below the Dinaric mountains.

17
00:02:17,796 --> 00:02:26,028
"Under Gorica", the small hill above it. From 1946 to 1992 it was called
Titograd.

18
00:02:26,688 --> 00:02:42,176
Remember Podgorica as the town under the little hill, renamed Titograd from
1946 to 1992, and crossed since 2005 by a cable-stayed Millennium Bridge.

19
00:02:42,847 --> 00:02:44,716
Let's bring Skopje into view.

20
00:02:48,078 --> 00:02:55,067
Skopje stands on the Vardar river in the north of North Macedonia, in the
middle of the Balkans.

21
00:02:55,729 --> 00:03:06,561
Roman Scupi. An earthquake destroyed most of it in 1963, and the Japanese
architect Kenzo Tange drew the plan for the new city.

22
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:16,520
Remember Skopje as Roman Scupi, rebuilt after the 1963 earthquake to a plan by
Kenzo Tange, and the birthplace of Mother Teresa.

23
00:03:17,204 --> 00:03:19,503
Let's bring Sarajevo into view.

24
00:03:23,588 --> 00:03:32,504
Sarajevo lies in a valley in central Bosnia and Herzegovina, on the Miljacka
river, ringed by the Dinaric mountains.

25
00:03:33,154 --> 00:03:44,033
From Turkish saray, "palace", and ova, "field": the plain in front of the
governor's palace. It hosted the Winter Olympics in 1984.

26
00:03:44,693 --> 00:03:59,020
Remember Sarajevo as the palace field that hosted the 1984 Winter Olympics,
and whose siege from 1992 to 1996 was the longest of a capital city in modern
war.

27
00:03:59,694 --> 00:04:01,819
Let's bring Sofia into view.

28
00:04:04,930 --> 00:04:13,196
Sofia lies in the west of Bulgaria, in a bowl of mountains with the Vitosha
ridge rising at its edge.

29
00:04:13,848 --> 00:04:23,182
Named for its church of Saint Sophia, "wisdom". The Romans called it Serdica,
and it has been the capital since 1879.

30
00:04:23,832 --> 00:04:33,677
Remember Sofia as the wisdom of Saint Sophia and the old Roman Serdica, with
the mountain of Vitosha standing at the city's edge.

31
00:04:34,342 --> 00:04:37,198
Let's bring Bucharest into view.

32
00:04:39,413 --> 00:04:47,041
Bucharest lies in the south-east of Romania, in the Wallachian plain, about
sixty kilometres north of the Danube.

33
00:04:47,695 --> 00:04:54,940
By legend named for a shepherd called Bucur. Between the wars it was called
Little Paris.

34
00:04:55,601 --> 00:05:06,561
Remember Bucharest as the shepherd's town that became Little Paris, with the
Romanian Athenaeum of 1888 paid for largely by public subscription.

35
00:05:10,633 --> 00:05:24,112
Tirana and Pristina are the southern pair, Belgrade the great river capital in
the north, with Podgorica, Skopje and Sarajevo around it, and Sofia and
Bucharest to the east.

36
00:05:24,776 --> 00:05:42,452
Remember the mosque and bazaar of Tirana, the NEWBORN monument of Pristina,
the white city at two rivers, the little hill of Podgorica, Kenzo Tange's
Skopje, the palace field of Sarajevo, the wisdom of Saint Sophia, and the
shepherd's Little Paris.
